# No `**/` needed on these, unlike their .dockerignore counterparts: git's own
# ignore-pattern semantics (not Docker's filepath.Match-based one) already match a
# slash-less pattern like `__pycache__/` or `*.pyc` at ANY depth by default — only a
# pattern containing a slash in the middle is anchored to the directory the
# .gitignore file lives in. Confirmed: `git check-ignore -v
# src/mcp104/tools/__pycache__/foo.pyc` and `src/mcp104.egg-info/PKG-INFO` both
# resolve to these bare rules. The two ignore files look like they share a pattern
# language; they do not — do not carry a `**/` decision from one file to the other in
# either direction.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.env
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# `pip install -e .` / `pip install --no-deps .` build metadata and in-tree build
# output — regenerated on every install, not source. `build/` is what setuptools
# leaves behind (a second copy of the package under build/lib/) if a build is ever run
# outside the Dockerfile's own same-layer cleanup; `dist/` is sdist/wheel output,
# never produced by this project's install/build commands today but excluded
# pre-emptively for the same reason.
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# Runtime data — never commit. cookies.json holds live 104 session cookies.
data/*.db
data/*.json
# *.sqlite as well: the extension list, not the directory, is what excludes these,
# so a file this project writes under a third extension would be committable. A
# verification script left three verify_*.sqlite here and git offered to add them.
data/*.sqlite

# Measurement captures — real candidates' résumés, names and e-mail addresses
# scraped from a live 104 enterprise session. Never commit. The probe scripts in
# research/probes/ ARE committed: they are the reproducible method and hold no
# personal data. See research/README.md.
research/captures/

# research/results/ is deliberately NOT ignored, and the split is structural
# rather than a per-file exception. `captures/` holds RAW responses, which carry
# other people's résumés; `results/` holds DERIVED measurements — totals, ratios,
# echoed parameter values, our own filter inputs — which carry none.
#
# The distinction was learned the hard way twice, in opposite directions. A
# results file was first ignored by inheriting the captures rule, which made the
# corpus behind a safety-relevant decision unavailable on a clean clone — the
# same unauditability that had just been diagnosed one round earlier, reproduced
# by the fix for it. Then a pattern-based sweep of that same file reported four
# "phone numbers" that were digit runs inside floating-point ratios.
#
# So: decide by WHICH FIELDS a file can contain, never by what its bytes look
# like. A regex over digits under-detects a person with no e-mail address and
# over-detects a decimal, and this project has now done both.
#
# The certification corpus that motivated this rule (certify_conditions_results.json)
# has SINCE moved out of this directory, to src/mcp104/assets/certification/ (the
# package-layout restructure — setuptools can only ship data that lives inside the
# package it belongs to). This rule still stands for what remains here
# (messaging_endpoints.json) and for anything future work adds to this directory, for
# the identical reason: derived measurements, no personal data.
